---
import DocLayout from "../../layouts/DocLayout.astro";
import { routeFor } from "../../i18n";
---

<DocLayout
  lang="en"
  page="docs/links"
  title="Link rules"
  intro="Opaline treats links as durable relationships between notes, headings, blocks, files, and concepts."
  next={[
    { href: routeFor("en", "docs/html-format"), label: "HTML format" },
    { href: routeFor("en", "docs/roadmap"), label: "Roadmap" },
  ]}
>
  <section>
    <h2>Two link layers</h2>
    <p>Ordinary HTML links remain the web-native base. Opaline can also recognize title shortcuts and structured relationships when they are useful inside the workbench.</p>
    <div class="doc-grid">
      <article class="doc-card">
        <h3>Title shortcuts</h3>
        <p>Fast note-to-note references can be resolved against local document titles while remaining readable text.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="doc-card">
        <h3>HTML links</h3>
        <p>Standard <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> links keep documents portable and browser-readable.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Relationship levels</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>File level:</strong> one document references another document.</li>
      <li><strong>Heading level:</strong> a link points to a section of a document.</li>
      <li><strong>Block level:</strong> a stable block ID points to a specific paragraph or component.</li>
      <li><strong>Concept level:</strong> tags or named ideas connect related material.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Workbench views</h2>
    <p>Links from this note, Mentions this note, broken-link detection, and the Relationship map all use these relationships. The goal is to make the workspace understandable without locking meaning inside one UI.</p>
  </section>
</DocLayout>
